1
00:00:00,438 --> 00:00:02,272
Previously on "The Ark"...

2
00:00:02,273 --> 00:00:03,648
Their message sounded
like they're in trouble.

3
00:00:03,649 --> 00:00:05,233
You're heading down there.
Is it safe?

4
00:00:05,234 --> 00:00:07,527
- Of course it's safe.
- They're all dead.

5
00:00:07,528 --> 00:00:09,571
Admiral Ben Hammond
of "Ark 4."

6
00:00:09,572 --> 00:00:12,490
Meet Milan Petrovic,
head of Homebase 1 security.

7
00:00:12,491 --> 00:00:14,159
I believe Kelly Fowler
should be freed.

8
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:15,910
I have to go out
in the exploration zone.

9
00:00:15,911 --> 00:00:17,954
You want to come?

10
00:00:17,955 --> 00:00:19,539
We're pinned down
and won't be able to make it

11
00:00:19,540 --> 00:00:21,332
to the shuttle.
- "Pinned down"? By what?

12
00:00:21,333 --> 00:00:22,834
Alien bloody creatures, mate.

13
00:00:24,378 --> 00:00:26,046
I'm gonna pull 'em off
this way.

14
00:00:26,047 --> 00:00:28,590
No!

15
00:00:28,591 --> 00:00:31,634
- Brice!
- Run!

16
00:00:31,635 --> 00:00:35,556
I'm so sorry, Eva,
but he's dead.

17
00:00:56,285 --> 00:00:58,328
I wish we had a funeral
for Lieutenant Brice

18
00:00:58,329 --> 00:01:01,539
like we did for Doc Morozov.

19
00:01:01,540 --> 00:01:03,375
Brice always thought
he was gonna die young

20
00:01:03,376 --> 00:01:06,044
from Klampkins and made it
very clear he didn't want us

21
00:01:06,045 --> 00:01:09,255
wallowing in grief for him.

22
00:01:09,256 --> 00:01:12,425
And yet here we are.

23
00:01:12,426 --> 00:01:14,969
Well, I mean, I didn't know
him as well as you lot did,

24
00:01:14,970 --> 00:01:17,597
but Brice always seemed like
a "raise a glass of scotch

25
00:01:17,598 --> 00:01:21,351
and celebrate me" kind of guy.
- No, no.

26
00:01:21,352 --> 00:01:24,688
A whiskey from the homeland
tasted like piss.

27
00:01:25,731 --> 00:01:27,899
Which do you think
he hated more,

28
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:30,068
Scotch or William Trust?

29
00:01:35,449 --> 00:01:39,577
Hammond to senior staff.
Report to the bridge.

30
00:01:39,578 --> 00:01:42,539
Can he do that,
just call a meeting?

31
00:01:42,540 --> 00:01:45,000
Apparently, he thinks he can.

32
00:01:53,634 --> 00:01:57,012
So I've been
reviewing your logs

33
00:01:57,013 --> 00:01:58,806
and I--

34
00:02:02,226 --> 00:02:04,519
What are you looking at?

35
00:02:04,520 --> 00:02:05,895
Uh, sir.

36
00:02:10,109 --> 00:02:12,235
Sorry, it was
the only free cabin.

37
00:02:12,236 --> 00:02:14,237
Your lieutenant
was the same size.

38
00:02:14,238 --> 00:02:17,615
As I said, I read your logs,

39
00:02:17,616 --> 00:02:19,617
and I want to set course
for the Tyson system.

40
00:02:19,618 --> 00:02:22,287
Wait, hold on.
Who's in charge?

41
00:02:22,288 --> 00:02:24,581
Sharon, obviously.

42
00:02:24,582 --> 00:02:28,251
I respect your
field promotion, Captain,

43
00:02:28,252 --> 00:02:29,961
but I still outrank you.

44
00:02:29,962 --> 00:02:32,922
In what?
The GSA is gone.

45
00:02:32,923 --> 00:02:34,549
Excuse me?

46
00:02:34,550 --> 00:02:36,676
You know
the GSA charter, Admiral.

47
00:02:36,677 --> 00:02:39,637
It clearly states we were to
get humanity to new homeworlds

48
00:02:39,638 --> 00:02:42,766
and relinquish control
to civilian governance.

49
00:02:45,644 --> 00:02:47,645
Then how does this all work?

50
00:02:47,646 --> 00:02:52,192
"Ark 1" is crewed by a mix
of former GSA and EF volunteers

51
00:02:52,193 --> 00:02:54,736
who've chosen to follow
Captain Garnet.

52
00:02:54,737 --> 00:02:56,863
"Ark 1" is now
a Homebase 1 ship,

53
00:02:56,864 --> 00:02:58,782
not a GSA ship.

54
00:03:03,245 --> 00:03:05,038
I'm sorry I overstepped.

55
00:03:05,039 --> 00:03:07,166
It won't happen again.
- Good.

56
00:03:09,085 --> 00:03:10,628
But if I may?

57
00:03:12,880 --> 00:03:15,423
Did you know that
when Evelyn Maddox fled

58
00:03:15,424 --> 00:03:18,051
Homebase 1, she set a course
in the shuttle nav system?

59
00:03:18,052 --> 00:03:19,344
Yes.

60
00:03:19,345 --> 00:03:20,970
So why haven't you
investigated?

61
00:03:20,971 --> 00:03:23,181
I thought it was
more important to make contact

62
00:03:23,182 --> 00:03:24,349
with the Ross colony.

63
00:03:24,350 --> 00:03:26,393
Even though
I warned you not to come?

64
00:03:26,394 --> 00:03:28,353
Well, that's a weird way
to say thanks.

65
00:03:28,354 --> 00:03:30,438
"Ark 1's" mission
is to save people at risk

66
00:03:30,439 --> 00:03:32,023
and bring them to Homebase.

67
00:03:32,024 --> 00:03:33,441
Okay, and don't you think

68
00:03:33,442 --> 00:03:35,360
there might be people
where Maddox was going?

69
00:03:35,361 --> 00:03:37,195
Yeah, people
that want to kill us.

70
00:03:37,196 --> 00:03:40,365
Or people who are suffering,
just as the people of Homebase

71
00:03:40,366 --> 00:03:43,535
were suffering
before you intervened.

72
00:03:43,536 --> 00:03:46,163
So you want
to investigate now?

73
00:03:49,208 --> 00:03:52,377
Tyson 5E.
It can't support life.

74
00:03:52,378 --> 00:03:54,838
So it's likely
a rendezvous point.

75
00:03:54,839 --> 00:03:56,089
Exactly.

76
00:03:56,090 --> 00:03:58,299
Whoever Maddox was meeting

77
00:03:58,300 --> 00:04:00,802
might still be there
waiting for her.

78
00:04:00,803 --> 00:04:04,347
We can drop out of FTL here,
right behind Tyson's moon.

79
00:04:04,348 --> 00:04:06,433
To hide us
from any ships in orbit.

80
00:04:06,434 --> 00:04:07,935
Solid plan.

81
00:04:11,397 --> 00:04:14,023
Okay, let's set a course.

82
00:04:19,155 --> 00:04:20,573
Admiral, a moment?

83
00:04:22,742 --> 00:04:25,535
You read my logs.
I read your files.

84
00:04:25,536 --> 00:04:27,537
I appreciate your experience.

85
00:04:27,538 --> 00:04:31,041
- But not enough to follow me?
- No.

86
00:04:31,042 --> 00:04:34,586
But you'd clearly be an asset.

87
00:04:34,587 --> 00:04:36,337
I could use a number two.

88
00:04:50,853 --> 00:04:52,312
Kelly?

89
00:04:52,313 --> 00:04:56,066
Where are--

90
00:04:56,067 --> 00:04:58,193
What am I wearing?

91
00:04:58,194 --> 00:05:00,153
I don't know.

92
00:05:00,154 --> 00:05:02,447
- You changed my clothes?
- No.

93
00:05:02,448 --> 00:05:04,449
I just woke up a minute ago.

94
00:05:05,868 --> 00:05:08,244
Why aren't you wearing
these weird clothes?

95
00:05:08,245 --> 00:05:10,080
Again, I don't know.

96
00:05:10,081 --> 00:05:11,498
We were in the woods.

97
00:05:11,499 --> 00:05:15,669
There was a flash of light,
and here we are.

98
00:05:21,842 --> 00:05:24,219
No signal.

99
00:05:24,220 --> 00:05:25,387
Okay.

100
00:05:29,225 --> 00:05:32,477
My implants are on, and
I'm not even making a scratch.

101
00:05:32,478 --> 00:05:35,438
There's no doors.
How did we get in here?

102
00:05:35,439 --> 00:05:37,232
What I want to know is...

103
00:05:40,111 --> 00:05:42,987
How do we get out?

104
00:06:02,925 --> 00:06:05,593
Captain Garnet,
Ian and I have a report on--

105
00:06:05,594 --> 00:06:08,054
Crewman, brief me first.

106
00:06:08,055 --> 00:06:09,597
- Oh.
- Seriously?

107
00:06:09,598 --> 00:06:11,725
Chain of command.

108
00:06:11,726 --> 00:06:14,894
As her second, it's my job to
make sure her life is easier.

109
00:06:14,895 --> 00:06:17,105
"Her second"?

110
00:06:17,106 --> 00:06:19,649
Thank you, but we do things
a little differently here.

111
00:06:19,650 --> 00:06:21,651
They can report to me directly.

112
00:06:21,652 --> 00:06:25,071
Captain, I gave up sunlight
and fresh air at Homebase

113
00:06:25,072 --> 00:06:27,407
to take orders from you.
- And I'm still in command.

114
00:06:27,408 --> 00:06:31,078
But you gave Brice's job
to some guy who hitched a ride?

115
00:06:34,081 --> 00:06:37,459
Let's all remember,
the admiral earned his rank.

116
00:06:37,460 --> 00:06:41,046
We can benefit
from his experience.

117
00:06:41,047 --> 00:06:43,423
My training is
in peaceful missions,

118
00:06:43,424 --> 00:06:45,593
same as Captain Garnet.

119
00:06:47,678 --> 00:06:50,305
Is that good enough
for you, crewman?

120
00:06:56,645 --> 00:06:58,563
You have a report
for the captain?

121
00:06:58,564 --> 00:07:02,067
Oh, uh, yeah,
um, we've been analyzing

122
00:07:02,068 --> 00:07:04,027
the data on the solar shields
we created at Ross.

123
00:07:04,028 --> 00:07:05,695
I think we can make them
sustainable.

124
00:07:05,696 --> 00:07:07,072
Yeah, with a bit of work,

125
00:07:07,073 --> 00:07:08,865
we can calibrate them
to protect us

126
00:07:08,866 --> 00:07:10,533
from different wavelengths
of radiation

127
00:07:10,534 --> 00:07:12,160
and electromagnetic pulses.

128
00:07:12,161 --> 00:07:15,038
And maybe even small impacts
from solid matter

129
00:07:15,039 --> 00:07:16,498
like asteroid fragments.

130
00:07:16,499 --> 00:07:18,375
How about impact
from artillery?

131
00:07:18,376 --> 00:07:20,502
Well, what ever happened
to peaceful missions?

132
00:07:20,503 --> 00:07:21,961
I want peace.

133
00:07:21,962 --> 00:07:24,131
Doesn't mean
everyone else does.

134
00:07:37,478 --> 00:07:38,978
Uh-oh.

135
00:07:38,979 --> 00:07:40,230
I know that look.

136
00:07:40,231 --> 00:07:41,940
This?

137
00:07:41,941 --> 00:07:44,526
This is my normal,
handsome, everyday look.

138
00:07:44,527 --> 00:07:46,778
It's your
"dying to say something

139
00:07:46,779 --> 00:07:49,739
but biting your tongue" look.

140
00:07:49,740 --> 00:07:51,324
No, it's not my place.

141
00:07:51,325 --> 00:07:53,118
I want to respect
your boundaries.

142
00:07:53,119 --> 00:07:57,080
Ha, says the man trying
to nickname me Curly

143
00:07:57,081 --> 00:07:58,748
because he dreamed it.

144
00:07:58,749 --> 00:08:02,419
Yeah, about that...

145
00:08:02,420 --> 00:08:04,129
I think I understand now

146
00:08:04,130 --> 00:08:07,924
why you think our time
in the coma wasn't real.

147
00:08:07,925 --> 00:08:11,052
I spent decades
learning astrophysics, right?

148
00:08:11,053 --> 00:08:13,430
Now it turns out
it was just my brain

149
00:08:13,431 --> 00:08:16,141
creating fake science.

150
00:08:16,142 --> 00:08:18,853
So what else
did my brain make up?

151
00:08:21,439 --> 00:08:23,690
I appreciate that.

152
00:08:23,691 --> 00:08:26,151
But I still want to know
what's on your mind.

153
00:08:26,152 --> 00:08:28,445
Isn't it obvious?

154
00:08:28,446 --> 00:08:29,904
I don't trust Hammond.

155
00:08:29,905 --> 00:08:31,781
Because he tried
to take command?

156
00:08:31,782 --> 00:08:34,909
Because he backed down
too easily.

157
00:08:34,910 --> 00:08:38,163
I mean, does that sound
like any admiral you ever met?

158
00:08:47,506 --> 00:08:52,177
Leaving FTL in three, two...

159
00:08:52,178 --> 00:08:54,429
now.

160
00:08:54,430 --> 00:08:57,891
Well, you cut that
a little close, Kimi.

161
00:08:57,892 --> 00:09:00,643
Plan called for us to get as
near to the moon as possible.

162
00:09:00,644 --> 00:09:02,145
And we got here in one piece.

163
00:09:02,146 --> 00:09:05,023
Call that good work,
Lieutenant Joma.

164
00:09:05,024 --> 00:09:07,025
Passive scans only, Alicia.

165
00:09:07,026 --> 00:09:10,403
Let's try not to alert anyone
to our presence.

166
00:09:10,404 --> 00:09:13,740
It might be
a little bit late for that.

167
00:09:13,741 --> 00:09:16,576
It's "Ark 22."
They have energy weapons.

168
00:09:16,577 --> 00:09:18,411
You remember what I said
about people

169
00:09:18,412 --> 00:09:20,580
wanting to kill us?
- Can your shield stop them?

170
00:09:20,581 --> 00:09:22,582
No, we're still
upgrading them.

171
00:09:22,583 --> 00:09:24,334
We're being hailed.

172
00:09:24,335 --> 00:09:25,585
Charge FTL fast.

173
00:09:25,586 --> 00:09:27,045
Send them to my screen.

174
00:09:32,385 --> 00:09:34,678
Oh.

175
00:09:34,679 --> 00:09:36,429
Smart hiding place.

176
00:09:36,430 --> 00:09:38,431
Didn't work but smart.

177
00:09:38,432 --> 00:09:41,059
Let me guess--you're looking
for Evelyn Maddox?

178
00:09:41,060 --> 00:09:42,060
Not exactly.

179
00:09:42,061 --> 00:09:44,062
I'm Captain Sharon Garnet
of "Ark 1."

180
00:09:44,063 --> 00:09:46,439
Kurtis Drake,
leader of "Ark 22."

181
00:09:46,440 --> 00:09:48,900
We were investigating
why Evelyn Maddox may--

182
00:09:48,901 --> 00:09:51,152
You're a clone,

183
00:09:51,153 --> 00:09:54,739
you and the gentleman
at your side.

184
00:09:54,740 --> 00:09:57,242
Uh, what makes you think
I'm a clone?

185
00:09:57,243 --> 00:10:00,787
Well, because
I'm married to you.

186
00:10:00,788 --> 00:10:05,208
I'm married to one of you.

187
00:10:05,209 --> 00:10:09,212
- This is, um, Jill.
- Hi, there.

188
00:10:09,213 --> 00:10:11,423
Is it conceited to say
you're very beautiful?

189
00:10:11,424 --> 00:10:15,969
Even in those drab colors.

190
00:10:15,970 --> 00:10:18,638
Oh, and we do have J6-82s
on board as well.

191
00:10:18,639 --> 00:10:21,891
Richard, Clark,
come and say hi.

192
00:10:21,892 --> 00:10:24,561
Oh.
Hello, brother.

193
00:10:24,562 --> 00:10:25,770
Hey.
- Yeah.

194
00:10:25,771 --> 00:10:27,647
Did you know there were
other clones of you?

195
00:10:27,648 --> 00:10:29,274
N--

196
00:10:29,275 --> 00:10:31,609
Come on over.
It'd be lovely to have you.

197
00:10:31,610 --> 00:10:34,529
No, we don't give up
our people.

198
00:10:34,530 --> 00:10:38,283
You misunderstand.
This is not a request.

199
00:10:38,284 --> 00:10:41,202
He's charging weapons.

200
00:10:41,203 --> 00:10:42,829
Fire on my mark.

201
00:10:42,830 --> 00:10:46,332
Three, two, one.

202
00:10:46,333 --> 00:10:48,501
- All right, we'll go.
- We'll come. We'll come over.

203
00:10:48,502 --> 00:10:50,003
Good.

204
00:10:50,004 --> 00:10:51,671
I look forward to it.

205
00:10:51,672 --> 00:10:54,300
Don't take too long.

206
00:10:56,719 --> 00:10:59,637
No, you can't go over there.

207
00:10:59,638 --> 00:11:01,348
We don't have a choice.

208
00:11:03,434 --> 00:11:06,561
Admiral Hammond has command
while I'm gone.

209
00:11:06,562 --> 00:11:07,854
Really?

210
00:11:07,855 --> 00:11:10,607
Those are my orders.

211
00:11:10,608 --> 00:11:12,192
Keep this ship safe.

212
00:11:17,156 --> 00:11:19,199
This could be
a good thing, right?

213
00:11:19,200 --> 00:11:21,493
I mean, means there's
more of us in the universe.

214
00:11:21,494 --> 00:11:23,953
Who have a whole lot
of weapons pointed at us.

215
00:11:23,954 --> 00:11:26,040
Yeah, there is that.

216
00:11:27,792 --> 00:11:29,125
I don't like this.

217
00:11:29,126 --> 00:11:32,253
We should have hidden weapons
in the shuttle.

218
00:11:32,254 --> 00:11:34,798
Weapons or not,
they're outnumbered.

219
00:11:34,799 --> 00:11:36,841
Clear of "Ark 1."

220
00:11:36,842 --> 00:11:39,219
Godspeed, Captain.

221
00:11:39,220 --> 00:11:41,346
"Ark 1," out.

222
00:11:41,347 --> 00:11:45,558
Okay, so what's your plan
to get them home?

223
00:11:45,559 --> 00:11:47,602
Is the FTL charged?

224
00:11:47,603 --> 00:11:49,854
Yeah, but--

225
00:11:51,357 --> 00:11:55,568
Ugh.
I really don't like that guy.

226
00:12:23,222 --> 00:12:25,432
It's a little early, Felix.

227
00:12:25,433 --> 00:12:29,310
Yes, well, I've been trying
to reach you since last night.

228
00:12:29,311 --> 00:12:31,896
My WD is off.
What do you need?

229
00:12:31,897 --> 00:12:33,481
Um...

230
00:12:43,784 --> 00:12:46,161
You're watching video
of Brice's death?

231
00:12:46,162 --> 00:12:48,038
Garnet sent me the footage

232
00:12:48,039 --> 00:12:50,498
from the shuttle's
hull cameras.

233
00:12:50,499 --> 00:12:51,834
Have you slept?

234
00:12:54,003 --> 00:12:56,421
Don't torture yourself
like this, Eva.

235
00:12:56,422 --> 00:12:58,840
I have to see it.

236
00:12:58,841 --> 00:13:01,551
May I ask you
to take a break?

237
00:13:01,552 --> 00:13:04,095
I have something to show you.

238
00:13:04,096 --> 00:13:05,430
Please.

239
00:13:08,809 --> 00:13:11,644
It's a new concept.
- Okay, what it is?

240
00:13:11,645 --> 00:13:12,938
There they are.

241
00:13:15,024 --> 00:13:16,274
Come here.

242
00:13:23,324 --> 00:13:25,033
I'm sorry.

243
00:13:28,829 --> 00:13:31,915
I don't normally like people
invading my personal space.

244
00:13:31,916 --> 00:13:34,250
However,
given the circumstances...

245
00:13:37,630 --> 00:13:40,215
He was a good man, I guess.

246
00:13:40,216 --> 00:13:43,176
So what did you have
to show me, Felix?

247
00:13:43,177 --> 00:13:45,261
That you are not alone.

248
00:13:47,973 --> 00:13:49,224
Thank you.

249
00:13:49,225 --> 00:13:50,558
Sit down.

250
00:13:54,188 --> 00:13:55,647
You eaten?

251
00:13:55,648 --> 00:13:58,316
You should eat.
- Stop mothering her.

252
00:13:58,317 --> 00:14:00,151
I'm doctoring her.

253
00:14:00,152 --> 00:14:02,821
I could definitely eat.
Who are we waiting for?

254
00:14:02,822 --> 00:14:05,323
Angus.
I couldn't get a hold of him.

255
00:14:05,324 --> 00:14:06,616
Neither could I.

256
00:14:06,617 --> 00:14:08,159
Well, I'm not gonna
wait for him.

257
00:14:08,160 --> 00:14:10,203
I'm gonna go
and get some breakfast.

258
00:14:10,204 --> 00:14:12,163
You do that.

259
00:14:12,164 --> 00:14:14,834
Hey, you want some coffee?

260
00:14:18,212 --> 00:14:21,840
Who do you think put us here?

261
00:14:21,841 --> 00:14:24,217
I'm more worried
about what put us here.

262
00:14:24,218 --> 00:14:25,260
Yeah, you have a point.

263
00:14:25,261 --> 00:14:27,011
These walls are too smooth
to be natural.

264
00:14:27,012 --> 00:14:28,930
They had to have been
machine-honed,

265
00:14:28,931 --> 00:14:31,933
which means someone
pretty advanced built these.

266
00:14:31,934 --> 00:14:36,020
God, I just--I wonder about
the light from these crystals.

267
00:14:36,021 --> 00:14:38,523
Is it electric,
naturally occurring,

268
00:14:38,524 --> 00:14:40,275
filtered through
from some different--

269
00:14:40,276 --> 00:14:41,735
Can you stop that?

270
00:14:41,736 --> 00:14:43,486
You said
it's not making a dent.

271
00:14:43,487 --> 00:14:45,114
Kelly, I said stop--

272
00:14:46,907 --> 00:14:50,578
Man, Kelly,
you're trashing your hand.

273
00:14:53,205 --> 00:14:55,832
It doesn't hurt that much.

274
00:14:55,833 --> 00:14:59,002
You're obviously in pain.
Take a break.

275
00:14:59,003 --> 00:15:02,255
It's not my hand that hurts.
It's my ears.

276
00:15:02,256 --> 00:15:05,216
With my implants on,
I hear this awful noise.

277
00:15:05,217 --> 00:15:06,468
Turn the implants off.

278
00:15:06,469 --> 00:15:08,428
Besides, you don't want to go
into organ failure.

279
00:15:08,429 --> 00:15:12,057
Whoever took us will be back.
I need to be ready to fight.

280
00:15:12,058 --> 00:15:14,684
I'll fight them off till
you get your implants back on.

281
00:15:14,685 --> 00:15:18,229
That's funny.

282
00:15:18,230 --> 00:15:20,315
Oh, you're serious.

283
00:15:20,316 --> 00:15:22,275
Turn them off.

284
00:15:25,654 --> 00:15:27,364
Better?

285
00:15:27,365 --> 00:15:29,157
I guess.

286
00:15:29,158 --> 00:15:31,826
At least I'm not hearing
that screeching sound anymore.

287
00:15:36,540 --> 00:15:39,501
Something's wrong.

288
00:15:39,502 --> 00:15:41,378
I'm still hearing those sounds.

289
00:15:41,379 --> 00:15:44,255
Wait, I hear them, too, now.

290
00:15:44,256 --> 00:15:47,842
Wait. That's what they sounded
like with your implants on?

291
00:15:47,843 --> 00:15:50,553
It's not that bad.

292
00:15:50,554 --> 00:15:53,181
Aah!

293
00:15:53,182 --> 00:15:55,017
Ah, strike that.

294
00:15:59,480 --> 00:16:02,190
And so I think we should take
a moment of silence

295
00:16:02,191 --> 00:16:05,402
for James Brice
and Dr. Morozov.

296
00:16:11,575 --> 00:16:13,660
Now we must move forward.

297
00:16:13,661 --> 00:16:17,163
I'm afraid we have
some more troubling news.

298
00:16:17,164 --> 00:16:20,875
Two of Homebase 1's citizens
are missing.

299
00:16:20,876 --> 00:16:24,921
Angus Medford and Kelly Fowler
went to the expanded zone

300
00:16:24,922 --> 00:16:26,214
and never returned.

301
00:16:26,215 --> 00:16:27,757
We lost contact.

302
00:16:27,758 --> 00:16:29,884
You know what
probably happened.

303
00:16:29,885 --> 00:16:32,178
Kelly went psycho
and killed her ex-boyfriend.

304
00:16:32,179 --> 00:16:34,055
Let's not jump
to conclusions.

305
00:16:34,056 --> 00:16:36,891
I spent 3 years
on "Ark 15" with her.

306
00:16:36,892 --> 00:16:39,602
We can't trust her.
- It's true!

307
00:16:41,313 --> 00:16:42,689
Stop.

308
00:16:42,690 --> 00:16:46,276
These speculations
about Ms. Fowler end now.

309
00:16:46,277 --> 00:16:48,570
She's proven
herself repeatedly.

310
00:16:48,571 --> 00:16:51,489
What's important
is to find them.

311
00:16:51,490 --> 00:16:54,368
I'm taking volunteers
for a search party.

312
00:16:56,787 --> 00:16:58,163
Thank you.

313
00:16:58,164 --> 00:17:00,790
We'll leave after
our last order of business.

314
00:17:00,791 --> 00:17:03,501
It's no secret that,
with the arrival

315
00:17:03,502 --> 00:17:06,421
of "Ark 1" and "EF2,"

316
00:17:06,422 --> 00:17:11,009
has led to some growing
problems in our colony.

317
00:17:11,010 --> 00:17:15,764
Our council has appointed
a new sheriff, Milan Petrovic.

318
00:17:19,894 --> 00:17:24,647
Some of you worked
with Milan on "Ark 11"

319
00:17:24,648 --> 00:17:26,399
when he was head of security.

320
00:17:26,400 --> 00:17:29,903
We're sure that he will do
as well at this position.

321
00:17:45,544 --> 00:17:47,545
Sanji?
Sanji, what's wrong?

322
00:17:47,546 --> 00:17:49,964
- It's him.
- Him--who?

323
00:17:49,965 --> 00:17:51,758
- The sheriff.
- What?

324
00:17:51,759 --> 00:17:53,969
He's my ex-husband.

325
00:17:58,724 --> 00:18:00,600
Hey, hey, are you crazy?

326
00:18:00,601 --> 00:18:01,976
Sanji, I'm gonna talk to him.

327
00:18:01,977 --> 00:18:03,228
- No.
- Sanji!

328
00:18:03,229 --> 00:18:05,647
No, I need to handle
this myself.

329
00:18:05,648 --> 00:18:07,273
No, I'll go.
I'll talk to him.

330
00:18:07,274 --> 00:18:09,902
Just give me
a moment to think.

331
00:18:12,488 --> 00:18:15,073
- Hello, Sanji.
- Stay away from her.

332
00:18:15,074 --> 00:18:17,909
- Damir.
- Let me talk to him.

333
00:18:17,910 --> 00:18:19,619
- I'll handle this.
- Sanji.

334
00:18:19,620 --> 00:18:20,996
Alone!

335
00:18:22,832 --> 00:18:24,249
Okay.

336
00:18:24,250 --> 00:18:26,543
I'll be in the dorm
if you need me.

337
00:18:33,634 --> 00:18:36,095
It's good to see you.

338
00:18:38,514 --> 00:18:42,517
Um, can we talk somewhere
more private?

339
00:18:42,518 --> 00:18:44,644
Here's fine.

340
00:18:44,645 --> 00:18:47,480
Fair enough.
Um...

341
00:18:47,481 --> 00:18:49,441
I owe you an apology.

342
00:18:52,820 --> 00:18:55,363
I'm sober now.

343
00:18:55,364 --> 00:18:59,492
I know that doesn't excuse
anything, but...

344
00:18:59,493 --> 00:19:03,413
I want you to know
I'm not the man I was.

345
00:19:03,414 --> 00:19:06,541
Good.

346
00:19:06,542 --> 00:19:10,628
I'm not the woman I was either.

347
00:19:10,629 --> 00:19:13,174
So leave me the hell alone.

348
00:19:33,194 --> 00:19:37,822
If you go near Sanji
ever again, I will end you.

349
00:19:37,823 --> 00:19:40,784
You hear me, you piece of shit?

350
00:19:40,785 --> 00:19:43,745
Loud and clear.

351
00:19:43,746 --> 00:19:46,498
What, you gonna hit me,
or you gonna let me go?

352
00:19:46,499 --> 00:19:48,625
What's it gonna be, hmm?

353
00:19:53,839 --> 00:19:55,757
Good to meet you, Damir.

354
00:20:09,188 --> 00:20:11,481
For someone so concerned
with GSA protocols...

355
00:20:11,482 --> 00:20:13,191
I can't believe you just
deserted Captain Garnet...

356
00:20:14,819 --> 00:20:16,736
That's a direct violation
of GSA codes.

357
00:20:16,737 --> 00:20:19,406
I guarantee you
no one will stand for it.

358
00:20:19,407 --> 00:20:22,117
Are you finished, or are you
just taking a breath?

359
00:20:22,118 --> 00:20:24,077
How can you joke right now?

360
00:20:24,078 --> 00:20:26,454
I'm actually trying
to keep my patience

361
00:20:26,455 --> 00:20:28,623
as you voice your concerns.

362
00:20:28,624 --> 00:20:31,000
You abandoned
Captain Garnet and Ian.

363
00:20:31,001 --> 00:20:33,128
You heard
the Captain's orders.

364
00:20:33,129 --> 00:20:37,215
She said keep the ship safe.
- Yeah, not run away.

365
00:20:37,216 --> 00:20:39,759
Ms. Nevins, your shields
still aren't repaired.

366
00:20:39,760 --> 00:20:41,636
What does that
have to do with--

367
00:20:41,637 --> 00:20:43,304
And we don't
have any weapons.

368
00:20:43,305 --> 00:20:45,473
- We weren't under attack.
- Drake had what he wanted.

369
00:20:45,474 --> 00:20:47,726
There was no reason to assume
that he wouldn't open up fire.

370
00:20:47,727 --> 00:20:49,602
And there's no reason
to assume that he would.

371
00:20:49,603 --> 00:20:52,105
I won't risk the lives of
the entire ship for two people!

372
00:20:52,106 --> 00:20:54,357
We just risked this entire
ship to save you on Ross.

373
00:20:54,358 --> 00:20:55,942
And that was a bad move!

374
00:20:55,943 --> 00:20:58,820
You lost your doctor
and your number two.

375
00:20:58,821 --> 00:21:01,156
The math doesn't work.

376
00:21:01,157 --> 00:21:04,659
People aren't math.

377
00:21:04,660 --> 00:21:06,746
Sometimes they are.

378
00:21:10,583 --> 00:21:14,294
On Ross, Garnet traded
two lives to save one.

379
00:21:14,295 --> 00:21:16,838
Math.

380
00:21:16,839 --> 00:21:18,673
She put me in charge
because she knew

381
00:21:18,674 --> 00:21:20,508
I wouldn't make
the same mistake.

382
00:21:20,509 --> 00:21:23,803
Or you're happy she's gone...

383
00:21:23,804 --> 00:21:26,264
so you can have this ship.

384
00:21:28,142 --> 00:21:31,186
Given the stress
of what's happened,

385
00:21:31,187 --> 00:21:33,980
I'm gonna pretend
I didn't hear that...

386
00:21:33,981 --> 00:21:35,565
Lieutenant Joma.

387
00:21:47,119 --> 00:21:49,079
Thank you so much
for coming over.

388
00:21:49,080 --> 00:21:51,206
Yeah, well,
we didn't have a choice.

389
00:21:51,207 --> 00:21:52,791
I never would have fired.

390
00:21:52,792 --> 00:21:55,293
Yeah, but sometimes
a show of force keeps people

391
00:21:55,294 --> 00:21:57,212
from getting the wrong idea.

392
00:21:57,213 --> 00:21:59,714
Oh, I see,
and the armed guards--

393
00:21:59,715 --> 00:22:02,217
what kind of idea is
that supposed to give us?

394
00:22:02,218 --> 00:22:04,135
Oh, just a precaution.

395
00:22:04,136 --> 00:22:06,721
I-I wanted to greet you
privately,

396
00:22:06,722 --> 00:22:10,225
but my wife--
yeah, she worries.

397
00:22:10,226 --> 00:22:12,602
WDs, please.

398
00:22:12,603 --> 00:22:14,145
Is that necessary?

399
00:22:14,146 --> 00:22:17,066
We'll keep them safe
in our security lockup.

400
00:22:25,282 --> 00:22:27,951
- Can you roll up your sleeves?
- Back off.

401
00:22:27,952 --> 00:22:31,287
It's okay.
We don't use J6-82s for organs.

402
00:22:31,288 --> 00:22:34,708
- I have a name, and it's Ian.
- Excellent.

403
00:22:34,709 --> 00:22:36,334
I encourage you even
to take a last name.

404
00:22:36,335 --> 00:22:37,794
What's that for?

405
00:22:37,795 --> 00:22:40,338
We need blood samples
to make sure you're not Gen-3s.

406
00:22:40,339 --> 00:22:41,756
Well, we're not.

407
00:22:41,757 --> 00:22:44,092
Otherwise, we would have
already developed psychosis.

408
00:22:44,093 --> 00:22:45,301
That's not true.

409
00:22:45,302 --> 00:22:47,887
There are the occasional
late bloomers.

410
00:22:47,888 --> 00:22:50,181
Easy way or hard way--
up to you.

411
00:22:54,770 --> 00:22:57,313
You two don't seem to know
much about clone science.

412
00:22:57,314 --> 00:22:58,606
We know enough.

413
00:22:58,607 --> 00:23:00,400
Original humans
are Generation 1,

414
00:23:00,401 --> 00:23:01,776
and us clones are Gen-2s.

415
00:23:01,777 --> 00:23:04,195
And clones made
from Gen-2 DNA are Gen-3.

416
00:23:04,196 --> 00:23:05,321
Very good.

417
00:23:05,322 --> 00:23:08,700
And Gen-3 brain cells
are unstable,

418
00:23:08,701 --> 00:23:11,327
and they do develop psychosis.

419
00:23:11,328 --> 00:23:12,620
They're dangerous,

420
00:23:12,621 --> 00:23:14,748
which is why we have
to make sure you aren't one.

421
00:23:14,749 --> 00:23:16,708
How do you know
Evelyn Maddox?

422
00:23:16,709 --> 00:23:18,668
Evelyn was
a business acquaintance.

423
00:23:18,669 --> 00:23:21,838
She sold me this Ark
during Earth's last days.

424
00:23:21,839 --> 00:23:25,050
Joke's on her.
What does money matter now?

425
00:23:25,051 --> 00:23:27,552
- Why were you meeting her?
- I have no idea.

426
00:23:27,553 --> 00:23:31,473
She called me, but something
tells me she, um...

427
00:23:31,474 --> 00:23:33,933
she won't be showing up
just now, will she?

428
00:23:33,934 --> 00:23:35,810
You could have told
us that over comms.

429
00:23:35,811 --> 00:23:37,645
Well, then I wouldn't have
the pleasure

430
00:23:37,646 --> 00:23:39,397
to meet you lovelies in person.

431
00:23:39,398 --> 00:23:40,648
What do you want from us?

432
00:23:40,649 --> 00:23:42,817
It's not what
I want from you.

433
00:23:42,818 --> 00:23:44,444
It's what you want from me.

434
00:23:50,785 --> 00:23:53,953
- Ian?
- Oh, I see it.

435
00:23:53,954 --> 00:23:55,830
I don't quite believe it.

436
00:23:55,831 --> 00:23:57,999
This ship is a safe haven
for clones.

437
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:00,836
Wait, are you saying everyone
on this ship is a clone?

438
00:24:03,005 --> 00:24:05,006
It's great, isn't it?

439
00:24:05,007 --> 00:24:08,259
Guys, how are you?

440
00:24:08,260 --> 00:24:10,178
Thank you so much for dinner.
- Ah!

441
00:24:10,179 --> 00:24:13,014
No, it was delicious.
It was perfect.

442
00:24:13,015 --> 00:24:15,642
I never imagined
so many clones survived Earth.

443
00:24:15,643 --> 00:24:17,477
It's amazing.

444
00:24:17,478 --> 00:24:19,979
Yeah, I mean, besides
my brothers in the lab,

445
00:24:19,980 --> 00:24:22,190
I've never even met
another clone like me.

446
00:24:22,191 --> 00:24:25,151
Same. I always thought
it was just me and Denise.

447
00:24:25,152 --> 00:24:28,071
I wonder how many
of each Gen-2 models were made.

448
00:24:28,072 --> 00:24:29,322
A lot.

449
00:24:29,323 --> 00:24:31,866
The original humans designed
clones to be exploited--

450
00:24:31,867 --> 00:24:34,160
you know, labor, parts, experimentation.

451
00:24:34,161 --> 00:24:36,079
So they wouldn't go
to all that expense

452
00:24:36,080 --> 00:24:38,123
only to create a few of us.
- That's right.

453
00:24:38,124 --> 00:24:40,583
Cloning was a business,
and if they hadn't cut corners

454
00:24:40,584 --> 00:24:42,210
by using Gen-2s
to create Gen-3s,

455
00:24:42,211 --> 00:24:44,045
the business
would have never been outlawed.

456
00:24:44,046 --> 00:24:45,755
I do have to admit,
it does make a nice change

457
00:24:45,756 --> 00:24:47,841
to see clones walking around
with smiles on their faces.

458
00:24:47,842 --> 00:24:49,926
So this is the part
where you thank me

459
00:24:49,927 --> 00:24:52,595
for saving you from that ship.
- Saving us?

460
00:24:52,596 --> 00:24:54,264
She was the freaking captain.

461
00:24:54,265 --> 00:24:55,932
Of a GSA Ark?

462
00:24:55,933 --> 00:24:57,934
They haven't exactly
been allies

463
00:24:57,935 --> 00:24:59,310
to the clone cause, have they?

464
00:24:59,311 --> 00:25:01,187
The GSA doesn't exist
anymore, mate.

465
00:25:01,188 --> 00:25:03,523
We've got a new colony now,
Homebase 1.

466
00:25:03,524 --> 00:25:06,151
It's pretty nice, too,
and clones are welcome there.

467
00:25:06,152 --> 00:25:08,278
And valued,
just like anyone else,

468
00:25:08,279 --> 00:25:10,822
just like we are welcome
on "Ark 1."

469
00:25:10,823 --> 00:25:13,283
Oh, okay.

470
00:25:13,284 --> 00:25:15,493
Then why did they
leave you behind?

471
00:25:25,504 --> 00:25:27,255
Working on a rescue plan?

472
00:25:27,256 --> 00:25:29,424
If the admiral wants math,
I'll give him math.

473
00:25:29,425 --> 00:25:32,427
I've served under men
like Hammond before.

474
00:25:32,428 --> 00:25:34,429
He's not changing his mind.

475
00:25:34,430 --> 00:25:37,182
I wish Lieutenant Brice
was still here.

476
00:25:37,183 --> 00:25:39,559
What gives him the right
to decide how we proceed?

477
00:25:39,560 --> 00:25:42,270
Garnet did
when she left him in charge.

478
00:25:42,271 --> 00:25:46,024
Well, he'll change
his tune when I show him this.

479
00:25:46,025 --> 00:25:49,569
I hope so,
but, um, just in case...

480
00:25:49,570 --> 00:25:51,197
here's a plan B.

481
00:25:53,157 --> 00:25:55,534
It's always good to have
a backup plan.

482
00:25:57,912 --> 00:25:59,913
Are you searching
for inhabitable worlds

483
00:25:59,914 --> 00:26:01,206
to settle on?

484
00:26:01,207 --> 00:26:03,375
Who's to say
we haven't found one?

485
00:26:03,376 --> 00:26:06,294
- Is that your old man?
- Oh, no, no.

486
00:26:06,295 --> 00:26:08,755
That's, um, Kurtis Drake prime,

487
00:26:08,756 --> 00:26:11,383
the inventor
of cloning science.

488
00:26:11,384 --> 00:26:14,344
But in a way, he's the--
he's the father of us all.

489
00:26:14,345 --> 00:26:16,221
You're his clone.

490
00:26:16,222 --> 00:26:20,475
Yes, uh, but do not mistake
my work for the creator's.

491
00:26:20,476 --> 00:26:23,853
He sought wealth.
- And what do you seek?

492
00:26:23,854 --> 00:26:26,523
To build a place
for us to live in harmony

493
00:26:26,524 --> 00:26:29,776
without oppression
from the original humans.

494
00:26:29,777 --> 00:26:31,444
Clone society.

495
00:26:31,445 --> 00:26:34,781
Well, forget the fact
that your model was designed

496
00:26:34,782 --> 00:26:36,491
for the rigors of space

497
00:26:36,492 --> 00:26:38,702
or that you were made
to be an organ donor.

498
00:26:38,703 --> 00:26:40,829
Here we can be
whatever we want--

499
00:26:40,830 --> 00:26:43,790
doctors, scientists, poets.

500
00:26:43,791 --> 00:26:47,502
Here we're free.

501
00:26:47,503 --> 00:26:49,587
We have a colony
you could join.

502
00:26:49,588 --> 00:26:52,966
I mean, the two of you might
be accepted, but all of us?

503
00:26:52,967 --> 00:26:55,719
Old prejudices die hard,
Captain.

504
00:26:55,720 --> 00:26:58,763
Morgan to Drake.
Can you come to medbay?

505
00:26:58,764 --> 00:27:00,974
On my way, Doctor.

506
00:27:00,975 --> 00:27:03,685
Oh, here's the mess hall.

507
00:27:03,686 --> 00:27:05,270
The food is delicious.

508
00:27:05,271 --> 00:27:07,480
Go on in. Relax.
Have a meal.

509
00:27:07,481 --> 00:27:09,441
I'm sure you'll have
more questions later.

510
00:27:09,442 --> 00:27:12,111
- No guards?
- We have nothing to hide.

511
00:27:27,460 --> 00:27:29,627
Uh...

512
00:27:44,393 --> 00:27:47,646
- I figured it out.
- You figured out what?

513
00:27:49,565 --> 00:27:51,399
There are a lot
of calculations on here

514
00:27:51,400 --> 00:27:53,276
that fall outside
of my expertise.

515
00:27:53,277 --> 00:27:56,696
Alicia has a plan to save
Captain Garnet and Ian.

516
00:27:56,697 --> 00:27:58,656
Oh.
I'm listening.

517
00:27:58,657 --> 00:28:01,576
A while ago, William Trust
created a weaponized EM pulse.

518
00:28:01,577 --> 00:28:03,620
According to your logs,
it nearly fried your engines.

519
00:28:03,621 --> 00:28:06,206
Yes, but I figured out
how to re-create it

520
00:28:06,207 --> 00:28:08,750
in a way that we can fire off
three shots safely.

521
00:28:08,751 --> 00:28:11,336
Wow.
Three shots.

522
00:28:11,337 --> 00:28:12,837
And what about your shields?

523
00:28:12,838 --> 00:28:14,255
Have you found a way

524
00:28:14,256 --> 00:28:16,424
to recalibrate them
to withstand "Ark 22" weapons?

525
00:28:16,425 --> 00:28:18,259
- No, but--
- All right, stop.

526
00:28:18,260 --> 00:28:20,011
But if the EM
disables their weapons--

527
00:28:20,012 --> 00:28:21,513
You're dismissed.

528
00:28:21,514 --> 00:28:24,307
Sir, no one is happy we've
left two of our own behind.

529
00:28:24,308 --> 00:28:25,809
We've had this conversation.

530
00:28:25,810 --> 00:28:28,395
I'm not gonna risk the lives
of the entire ship on an if.

531
00:28:28,396 --> 00:28:31,022
- Is this your final decision?
- Yes, it's my final decision.

532
00:28:31,023 --> 00:28:33,316
Now, unless you can--

533
00:28:36,821 --> 00:28:39,989
I was really hoping
we wouldn't have to do plan B.

534
00:28:43,786 --> 00:28:48,581
No one in or out until
we've got our captain back.

535
00:28:51,627 --> 00:28:53,545
Time is not on our side.

536
00:28:53,546 --> 00:28:55,505
If Kelly and Angus
suffered an accident,

537
00:28:55,506 --> 00:28:57,716
they'll need food, water,
and medical attention.

538
00:28:57,717 --> 00:28:59,927
We should pair up
and widen our search.

539
00:29:02,304 --> 00:29:04,764
I'll search with you, Felix.

540
00:29:04,765 --> 00:29:07,225
I think not.

541
00:29:09,061 --> 00:29:11,855
Who told you, Sanji or Marsh?

542
00:29:11,856 --> 00:29:13,940
I would have never approved
you as sheriff

543
00:29:13,941 --> 00:29:15,400
if I'd known who you are.

544
00:29:15,401 --> 00:29:17,527
Who I am, Felix,

545
00:29:17,528 --> 00:29:21,740
is a guy who wants to help
you find two missing people.

546
00:29:21,741 --> 00:29:23,408
Hey.

547
00:29:23,409 --> 00:29:26,119
Do you want my help or not?

548
00:29:38,716 --> 00:29:42,053
This is a torture technique.

549
00:29:43,721 --> 00:29:46,347
My WD is picking up patterns
in the noise.

550
00:29:46,348 --> 00:29:48,516
There's harmonic variations
at frequencies

551
00:29:48,517 --> 00:29:50,310
outside the range
of human hearing.

552
00:29:50,311 --> 00:29:51,853
So what does that mean?

553
00:29:51,854 --> 00:29:53,980
It explains
why you could hear it

554
00:29:53,981 --> 00:29:57,817
when your implants were on.
- How is that helpful?

555
00:29:57,818 --> 00:30:00,612
I have no idea.

556
00:30:00,613 --> 00:30:02,740
I don't know.
I don't know.

557
00:30:18,923 --> 00:30:21,257
Oh, Markovic.
I'm glad you're here.

558
00:30:21,258 --> 00:30:23,968
I could use your help
to recalibrate the spectrograph

559
00:30:23,969 --> 00:30:26,638
to run a chemical analysis
on the tablet.

560
00:30:26,639 --> 00:30:29,849
Maybe later.

561
00:30:29,850 --> 00:30:31,226
This tablet

562
00:30:31,227 --> 00:30:34,062
could be the greatest discovery
of our lifetime,

563
00:30:34,063 --> 00:30:35,689
maybe human history.

564
00:30:35,690 --> 00:30:38,693
So what could possibly be
more interesting than that?

565
00:30:40,986 --> 00:30:43,488
Well...

566
00:30:43,489 --> 00:30:45,365
maybe working on the tablet

567
00:30:45,366 --> 00:30:47,200
will help take your mind
off your grief.

568
00:30:47,201 --> 00:30:50,537
Something happened to Brice
as the shuttle took off.

569
00:30:50,538 --> 00:30:53,748
- Eva.
- Just watch.

570
00:30:53,749 --> 00:30:56,710
First, an exhaust fume,

571
00:30:56,711 --> 00:30:58,753
and there, he's gone.

572
00:30:58,754 --> 00:31:00,755
I don't want to be harsh,

573
00:31:00,756 --> 00:31:03,717
but, uh, something
was eating him.

574
00:31:03,718 --> 00:31:06,636
- Then where is his body?
- I don't know.

575
00:31:06,637 --> 00:31:09,472
I guess the creature
dragged it away?

576
00:31:09,473 --> 00:31:13,893
Or he--he walked away.

577
00:31:13,894 --> 00:31:16,104
Eva...

578
00:31:16,105 --> 00:31:17,647
I understand.

579
00:31:17,648 --> 00:31:19,023
I really do.

580
00:31:19,024 --> 00:31:22,193
I desperately miss
Helena and Cat,

581
00:31:22,194 --> 00:31:24,863
but I've had to accept
that they're gone,

582
00:31:24,864 --> 00:31:27,574
just like you have to accept
that Lieutenant Brice is gone.

583
00:31:27,575 --> 00:31:29,617
Now, come on.

584
00:31:29,618 --> 00:31:32,412
Come and help me
with the tablet, please?

585
00:31:32,413 --> 00:31:33,621
No.

586
00:31:48,846 --> 00:31:51,431
Sun's getting low,
and it's still hot as hell.

587
00:31:51,432 --> 00:31:52,724
Everyone's exhausted.

588
00:31:52,725 --> 00:31:55,101
We can't stop
until we find them.

589
00:31:55,102 --> 00:31:56,394
Look, I get it.

590
00:31:56,395 --> 00:31:58,271
Angus and Kelly
are your people,

591
00:31:58,272 --> 00:32:01,858
but we've been at this all day.
- They're our people.

592
00:32:01,859 --> 00:32:03,568
Sure.

593
00:32:03,569 --> 00:32:04,986
But so are they.

594
00:32:04,987 --> 00:32:07,072
Look, everyone here
needs a meal

595
00:32:07,073 --> 00:32:08,156
and a good night's sleep.

596
00:32:08,157 --> 00:32:10,200
We can continue
the search tomorrow.

597
00:32:10,201 --> 00:32:12,994
Come on, man,
tell me you're not tired.

598
00:32:12,995 --> 00:32:16,707
I'm going to keep on looking.
You can do what you want.

599
00:32:18,751 --> 00:32:22,295
Well, you're a better man
than me, Felix.

600
00:32:22,296 --> 00:32:24,923
Okay, everyone who's ready
to call it for the night,

601
00:32:24,924 --> 00:32:26,257
follow me.

602
00:32:35,851 --> 00:32:38,103
I can't take
much more of this!

603
00:32:38,104 --> 00:32:39,229
What?

604
00:32:39,230 --> 00:32:41,481
I said, I can't take
more of this!

605
00:32:41,482 --> 00:32:43,024
Me either!

606
00:32:52,493 --> 00:32:54,745
Maybe someone heard us?

607
00:32:56,664 --> 00:32:59,124
Or the next torture
is about to begin.

608
00:33:03,754 --> 00:33:06,131
Get behind me.

609
00:33:09,552 --> 00:33:11,761
Maybe we're
supposed to go out?

610
00:33:11,762 --> 00:33:13,972
It could be a trap.

611
00:33:33,492 --> 00:33:35,702
Oh, shit.
- Angus!

612
00:33:42,710 --> 00:33:45,671
Maybe let's stay in here.

613
00:33:55,973 --> 00:33:57,807
Welcome aboard.

614
00:33:57,808 --> 00:33:59,350
- Ah.
- Thank you.

615
00:33:59,351 --> 00:34:01,186
Happy to be here.

616
00:34:01,187 --> 00:34:03,104
Something's wrong here, right?

617
00:34:03,105 --> 00:34:05,356
Yeah, Drake's pushing the
hard sell a little too much.

618
00:34:05,357 --> 00:34:07,192
Hey, girl!

619
00:34:07,193 --> 00:34:09,652
I've totally got clothes
in your size

620
00:34:09,653 --> 00:34:12,405
if you, like,
ever want to borrow them.

621
00:34:12,406 --> 00:34:14,532
Uh, y-you know I do.

622
00:34:14,533 --> 00:34:16,701
I knew it.

623
00:34:18,871 --> 00:34:21,081
Have you noticed there's
no other clones of Drake here,

624
00:34:21,082 --> 00:34:22,332
but there are of everyone else?

625
00:34:22,333 --> 00:34:23,792
Yeah, we're gonna need

626
00:34:23,793 --> 00:34:25,585
to figure out
his connection to Maddox,

627
00:34:25,586 --> 00:34:27,504
get our WDs,
and signal "Ark 1."

628
00:34:27,505 --> 00:34:29,005
You don't really believe

629
00:34:29,006 --> 00:34:30,799
we can rely
on Hammond for help?

630
00:34:30,800 --> 00:34:32,300
If we can make contact,

631
00:34:32,301 --> 00:34:34,135
uh, maybe we can figure out
a rescue plan

632
00:34:34,136 --> 00:34:35,762
that won't put "Ark 1"
in danger.

633
00:34:35,763 --> 00:34:37,514
Fair enough.

634
00:34:37,515 --> 00:34:39,307
Divide and conquer?

635
00:34:39,308 --> 00:34:41,309
Yes, I'll get
into the security lockup

636
00:34:41,310 --> 00:34:42,686
and grab our WDs.

637
00:34:42,687 --> 00:34:44,270
Right, I'll go
to the server room,

638
00:34:44,271 --> 00:34:46,022
see if there's
any communications logs

639
00:34:46,023 --> 00:34:47,482
with Maddox.
- I don't know.

640
00:34:47,483 --> 00:34:50,402
Going to the bridge
might look suspicious.

641
00:34:50,403 --> 00:34:52,612
What about Drake's quarters?

642
00:34:52,613 --> 00:34:55,615
Maybe there's a WD there
with some information on it.

643
00:34:55,616 --> 00:34:57,033
Good idea.

644
00:34:57,034 --> 00:34:58,869
Meet at the shuttle.
- Yeah.

645
00:35:03,916 --> 00:35:05,583
What have you got for me?

646
00:35:05,584 --> 00:35:08,336
I ran the blood sample
three times.

647
00:35:10,715 --> 00:35:15,218
We finally found
what we were looking for.

648
00:35:38,409 --> 00:35:40,702
- You're not Clark.
- Uh, yes, I am.

649
00:35:40,703 --> 00:35:43,329
No, you're not.

650
00:35:43,330 --> 00:35:46,541
No, I'm not.

651
00:35:46,542 --> 00:35:49,294
You're one of the clones
Kurtis freed from "Ark 1."

652
00:35:49,295 --> 00:35:51,338
"Freed"?

653
00:35:51,339 --> 00:35:53,382
Kidnapped, more like.

654
00:35:55,593 --> 00:35:57,385
Oh, no.

655
00:35:57,386 --> 00:35:59,637
No, I wouldn't do that
if I was you.

656
00:35:59,638 --> 00:36:01,806
Kurtis is gonna throw
your ass out the airlock

657
00:36:01,807 --> 00:36:04,893
for sneaking into our quarters.
- Oh, yeah?

658
00:36:04,894 --> 00:36:07,729
And tell me, what do you think
would make Drake angrier--

659
00:36:07,730 --> 00:36:09,064
me sneaking in here

660
00:36:09,065 --> 00:36:13,651
or you dressed like that
waiting for a guy named Clark?

661
00:36:17,114 --> 00:36:18,782
What do you want?

662
00:36:18,783 --> 00:36:21,451
What's Drake's connection
to Maddox?

663
00:36:21,452 --> 00:36:24,412
You tell me that,
and I'll leave.

664
00:36:24,413 --> 00:36:26,998
All's I know is Maddox
was trying to help Kurtis

665
00:36:26,999 --> 00:36:28,833
find a special G-44?

666
00:36:28,834 --> 00:36:30,668
What's a G-44?

667
00:36:33,381 --> 00:36:35,216
You're looking at one.

668
00:36:37,426 --> 00:36:40,262
Ship full of us
and still not the one he wants.

669
00:36:42,139 --> 00:36:45,266
Sharon.

670
00:36:50,439 --> 00:36:54,943
Oh, and you must be
the new G-44 clone.

671
00:36:54,944 --> 00:36:57,362
I'm Roger.

672
00:36:57,363 --> 00:36:59,907
Nice to meet you.

673
00:37:02,910 --> 00:37:05,078
Do you want to go
for a drink sometime?

674
00:37:05,079 --> 00:37:06,704
Excuse me?

675
00:37:06,705 --> 00:37:09,249
It's just that I find that
I'm most compatible with G-44s,

676
00:37:09,250 --> 00:37:10,834
and I don't mean to brag,

677
00:37:10,835 --> 00:37:14,546
but I usually sweep them
off their feet.

678
00:37:14,547 --> 00:37:16,089
I'll grab her legs.

679
00:37:16,090 --> 00:37:19,510
Let's get her to Dr. Morgan
before this wears off.

680
00:37:23,514 --> 00:37:26,474
Well, I guess everyone's
done playing nice.

681
00:37:26,475 --> 00:37:28,518
Okay, listen up, team.

682
00:37:28,519 --> 00:37:30,353
The moment we're out of FTL,

683
00:37:30,354 --> 00:37:32,605
the bridge will fire
an EM pulse at "Ark 22."

684
00:37:32,606 --> 00:37:34,566
Once we have confirmation
of a hit,

685
00:37:34,567 --> 00:37:36,276
we'll launch and board.

686
00:37:36,277 --> 00:37:39,571
We should expect
heavy resistance.

687
00:37:39,572 --> 00:37:41,781
Bridge to Kimi--
we're two minutes out.

688
00:37:41,782 --> 00:37:42,866
Are you ready?

689
00:37:42,867 --> 00:37:45,869
We're locked and loaded, bridge.

690
00:37:55,338 --> 00:37:57,088
Wow.

691
00:37:57,089 --> 00:37:59,424
You shouldn't be conscious.

692
00:37:59,425 --> 00:38:02,093
Our metabolism is fantastic,
isn't it?

693
00:38:02,094 --> 00:38:05,013
Triple the dosage
on the sedative.

694
00:38:07,767 --> 00:38:10,602
Don't worry.

695
00:38:10,603 --> 00:38:12,937
This won't hurt too much.

696
00:38:23,199 --> 00:38:26,159
Where's Garnet?

697
00:38:26,160 --> 00:38:28,954
- Medbay.
- Oh, yeah?

698
00:38:33,751 --> 00:38:36,461
- You don't have to do this.
- Yes, I do. You're the one.

699
00:38:36,462 --> 00:38:37,754
What does that mean?

700
00:38:39,507 --> 00:38:40,882
Shh.

701
00:38:40,883 --> 00:38:42,551
Go to sleep now.

702
00:38:44,637 --> 00:38:45,846
Sharon!

703
00:39:29,223 --> 00:39:30,433
Ian!

704
00:39:35,062 --> 00:39:37,398
Let's get the hell
off this ship.

705
00:39:39,108 --> 00:39:41,526
Sasha, you have a handle
on the EM pulse, right?

706
00:39:41,527 --> 00:39:43,153
- Yep.
- Good.

707
00:39:43,154 --> 00:39:45,030
Timing is everything on this.

708
00:39:45,031 --> 00:39:47,115
Leaving FTL

709
00:39:47,116 --> 00:39:50,744
in three, two, one...

710
00:39:50,745 --> 00:39:52,662
Fire EMP, Sasha.

711
00:39:52,663 --> 00:39:54,664
At what?

712
00:39:54,665 --> 00:39:56,875
There's no sign of "Ark 22."

713
00:39:56,876 --> 00:39:58,461
Oh, no.

714
00:40:01,339 --> 00:40:03,757
We can't get a visual
on "Ark 22."

715
00:40:03,758 --> 00:40:07,595
We're too late.
"Ark 22" has left.

716
00:40:13,726 --> 00:40:17,228
I asked you to do one thing.

717
00:40:17,229 --> 00:40:20,815
I know. I'm sorry.
I just saw red.

718
00:40:20,816 --> 00:40:22,901
That--that
can't happen again.

719
00:40:22,902 --> 00:40:25,153
It won't.
I-I promise.

720
00:40:25,154 --> 00:40:27,739
I need to handle this my way.

721
00:40:27,740 --> 00:40:30,116
I know.

722
00:40:30,117 --> 00:40:32,869
Last thing I would want
to do is hurt you.

723
00:40:32,870 --> 00:40:35,081
Okay?
- Okay.

724
00:40:36,791 --> 00:40:39,209
Yeah, I'm sorry.

725
00:40:45,841 --> 00:40:49,552
Why are you looking
at Milan's file?

726
00:40:49,553 --> 00:40:52,180
I just wanted to see
who we're dealing with.

727
00:40:52,181 --> 00:40:55,600
We are not dealing with this.
I am.

728
00:40:55,601 --> 00:40:58,645
Why can't you understand that?
- I do.

729
00:40:58,646 --> 00:41:00,688
I do. I'm s--

730
00:41:00,689 --> 00:41:01,773
Sanji.

731
00:41:01,774 --> 00:41:04,192
- I need some time alone.
- Oh, come on.

732
00:41:04,193 --> 00:41:05,610
I said I need some time.

733
00:41:05,611 --> 00:41:08,905
Or do I need to tell you
that twice, too?

734
00:41:11,617 --> 00:41:14,494
I tried to keep Felix
from searching too far

735
00:41:14,495 --> 00:41:18,623
from the settlement,
but he is determined

736
00:41:18,624 --> 00:41:21,543
to find Angus and Kelly.

737
00:41:21,544 --> 00:41:23,962
You need to take this
more seriously.

738
00:41:25,965 --> 00:41:28,800
I am taking this seriously,

739
00:41:28,801 --> 00:41:31,554
but there's nothing we can do.

740
00:41:33,431 --> 00:41:36,850
You could have stopped Felix
from searching further.

741
00:41:36,851 --> 00:41:41,062
Well, it's not like
he's gonna find the two kids.

742
00:41:41,063 --> 00:41:44,274
Ves, we both know
what happened to them.

743
00:41:44,275 --> 00:41:45,817
Do we?

744
00:41:45,818 --> 00:41:48,069
Because if you're right,

745
00:41:48,070 --> 00:41:50,697
we have a much bigger problem
than we thought.

